@node Naming system
@section Naming System

Lua uses @code{case-sensitive} naming with identifiers normalized to
@code{snake_case}. The lexer converts PascalCase and camelCase identifiers
to snake_case format.

@example
myVariable   @result{} my_variable
playerHealth @result{} player_health
SPRITE_COUNT @result{} sprite_count
@end example

@table @asis
@item Keywords
@code{and}, @code{break}, @code{do}, @code{else}, @code{elseif},
@code{end}, @code{false}, @code{for}, @code{function}, @code{if},
@code{in}, @code{local}, @code{nil}, @code{not}, @code{or},
@code{repeat}, @code{return}, @code{then}, @code{true}, @code{until},
@code{while}, @code{dialogue}, @code{dialog}, @code{print}, @code{input},
@code{copy}, @code{move}, @code{to}, @code{continue}

@item Operators
@code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{%}, @code{^},
@code{==}, @code{~=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=},
@code{and}, @code{or}, @code{not}, @code{#} (length)

@item Number Formats
Decimal (@code{123}), hexadecimal (@code{0xFF}), octal (@code{0o77}),
binary (@code{0b1010})

@item String Delimiters
Double quotes (@code{"..."}), single quotes (@code{'...'}), long brackets
(@code{[=[ ... ]=]})

@item Comments
Double hyphen to end of line (@code{-- comment}), block comment
(@code{--[[ block ]]})
@end table

@node Variables and declarations
@section Variables and Declarations

@findex local
@findex nil

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Variables in Lua are dynamically typed storage locations. The @code{local}
keyword declares variables with local scope (limited to the current block),
while global variables persist across the entire program.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
local @var{name} = @var{expression}
local @var{name1}, @var{name2}, @var{name3}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Basic assignment
local score = 0
local name = "Player"

-- Multiple declarations
local x, y, z

-- Initialize from expression
local distance = math.sqrt((x2-x1)^2 + (y2-y1)^2)

-- Uninitialized locals are nil
local pending
print(pending)  -- nil
@end example

@subsubheading Global Variables

Variables declared without @code{local} are global:

@example
score = 0           -- global
MAX_SPEED = 100     -- global constant (convention)
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: letters, digits, underscores; cannot start with digit
@item Scope: local to block, function, or entire program
@item Type: dynamically determined from assigned value
@end itemize

@subsubheading nil Value

@code{nil} represents the absence of a value:

@example
local uninitialized = nil
if value == nil then
    print("Not set")
end
@end example

@node Tables (arrays and dictionaries)
@section Tables (Arrays and Dictionaries)

@findex table

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Tables are Lua's sole data structure, combining the functionality of
arrays, dictionaries (hash maps), objects, and modules. They are
implemented as associative arrays with automatic key management.

@subsubheading Array Syntax

@example
@var{array} = @{ @var{value1}, @var{value2}, @var{...} @}
@var{array}[@var{index}]
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Simple array
local numbers = @{1, 2, 3, 4, 5@}
print(numbers[1])      -- 1
print(numbers[3])      -- 3

-- Mixed types
local mixed = @{"hello", 42, true, 3.14@}

-- Nested arrays
local matrix = @{
    @{1, 0, 0@},
    @{0, 1, 0@},
    @{0, 0, 1@}
@}
print(matrix[2][2])    -- 1
@end example

@subsubheading Dictionary Syntax

@example
@var{dict} = @{ key1 = @var{value1}, key2 = @var{value2} @}
@var{dict}.@var{key}
@var{dict}[@var{key}]
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Dictionary/object
local player = @{
    name = "Hero",
    health = 100,
    x = 10,
    y = 20
@}
print(player.name)        -- Hero
print(player["health"])   -- 100

-- Dynamic keys
local key = "score"
data[key] = 1000
@end example

@subsubheading Array Length

@example
#@var{array}        -- length operator
#@var{string}       -- also works for strings
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
local items = @{10, 20, 30@}
print(#items)           -- 3
for i = 1, #items do
    print(items[i])
end
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Array indices: 1 to N for arrays, any value for dictionaries
@item Keys: any non-nil value (strings, numbers, tables, functions)
@item Values: any Lua value including other tables
@end itemize

@node Functions
@section Functions

@findex function
@findex return
@findex local

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Functions are reusable blocks of code that perform specific tasks.
They can take parameters, return values, and be stored in variables.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
function @var{name}(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end

local function @var{name}(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end

@var{var} = function(@var{params})
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Simple function
function greet()
    print("Hello!")
end

-- Function with parameters
function add(a, b)
    return a + b
end

-- Function with multiple returns
function divide(a, b)
    return math.floor(a / b), a % b
end

-- Local function
local function calculate(x, y)
    return x * y + 10
end

-- Function stored in variable
local double = function(n)
    return n * 2
end
@end example

@subsubheading Parameters and Arguments

@example
function process(a, b, c)
    print(a, b, c)
end

process(1, 2, 3)         -- 1, 2, 3
process(1, 2)            -- 1, 2, nil
process(1, 2, 3, 4, 5)   -- 1, 2, 3 (extra args ignored)
@end example

@subsubheading Return Values

@example
return              -- no value
return @var{expr}   -- single value
return @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{...}  -- multiple values
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
function getStats()
    return score, health, mana
end

s, h, m = getStats()

-- Multiple returns with selection
function firstLast(list)
    return list[1], list[#list]
end

first, last = firstLast(@{1,2,3,4,5@})  -- first=1, last=5
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Function names: valid identifiers or table keys
@item Parameters: zero or more, comma-separated
@item Return values: zero or more
@item Closure: functions capture local variables from enclosing scope
@end itemize

@node Conditional execution
@section Conditional Execution

@findex if
@findex then
@findex else
@findex elseif
@findex end

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Conditional statements execute different code paths based on boolean
expressions. Lua supports simple if/then, if/then/else, and chained
elseif chains.

@subsubheading Syntax

@example
if @var{condition} then
    @var{statements}
end

if @var{condition} then
    @var{statements}
else
    @var{statements}
end

if @var{cond1} then
    @var{stmts1}
elseif @var{cond2} then
    @var{stmts2}
elseif @var{cond3} then
    @var{stmts3}
else
    @var{stmts4}
end
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Simple if
if score > 1000 then
    print("High score!")
end

-- If-else
if health <= 0 then
    gameOver = true
else
    health = health - damage
end

-- Elseif chain
if temperature > 30 then
    status = "hot"
elseif temperature > 15 then
    status = "warm"
elseif temperature > 0 then
    status = "cool"
else
    status = "cold"
end

-- Nested conditions
if player then
    if player.health > 0 then
        updatePlayer()
    end
end
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Inputs

@itemize @bullet
@item Any expression that evaluates to true or false
@item Nil and false are falsy; all other values are truthy
@item Use @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{not} for complex conditions
@end itemize

@subsubheading Outputs

The first branch with a true condition executes; if none, the else branch
(if present) executes.

@node Loop constructs
@section Loop Constructs

@findex while
@findex do
@findex end
@findex for
@findex repeat
@findex until
@findex break
@findex continue

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Loops repeat a block of statements either a fixed number of times,
while a condition holds, until a condition becomes true, or by iterating
over a sequence.

@subsubheading While Loop (PRE-TEST)

@example
while @var{condition} do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
local i = 1
while i <= 10 do
    print("Count:", i)
    i = i + 1
end

while running do
    processEvents()
    updateGame()
    if quit then break end
end
@end example

@subsubheading Repeat Loop (POST-TEST)

@example
repeat
    @var{statements}
until @var{condition}
@end example

The body executes at least once before the condition is checked.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
repeat
    print("Enter password:")
    input = read()
until input == "secret"

repeat
    i = i + 1
    process(i)
until i >= max
@end example

@subsubheading Numeric For Loop

@example
for @var{var} = @var{start}, @var{end}, @var{step} do
    @var{statements}
end
@end example

The step defaults to 1 if omitted.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Count 1 to 10
for i = 1, 10 do
    print(i)
end

-- Count with step
for i = 0, 100, 5 do
    print(i)
end

-- Count down
for i = 10, 1, -1 do
    print("T-minus", i)
end

-- Nested loops
for i = 1, 3 do
    for j = 1, 3 do
        print(i, j)
    end
end
@end example

@subsubheading Break Statement

@example
break
@end example

Exits the innermost loop immediately.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
for i = 1, 100 do
    if items[i] == target then
        print("Found at", i)
        break
    end
end
@end example

@subsubheading Continue Statement

@example
continue
@end example

Skips to the next iteration of the loop.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
for i = 1, 10 do
    if i % 2 == 0 then
        continue  -- skip even numbers
    end
    print(i)  -- only odd numbers printed
end
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Counter variable: any valid identifier
@item Start/end/step: numeric expressions
@item Step: can be positive or negative
@end itemize

@node Input and output
@section Input and Output

@findex print
@findex input
@findex dialogue
@findex dialog

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

I/O statements handle user interaction, displaying output, and reading
input values.

@subsubheading Print Statement

@example
print @var{expression}
print @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, ...
print @var{expr1} .. @var{expr2}    -- concatenation
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
print("Hello, World!")
print(score)
print("Score:", score, "Lives:", lives)
print("Result: " .. result)
print(x .. " + " .. y .. " = " .. (x + y))
@end example

@subsubheading Input Statement

@example
input @var{variable}
input @var{var1}, @var{var2}, ...
@end example

Reads input into one or more variables.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
input userName
input x, y, z
@end example

@subsubheading Dialogue Statement

@example
dialogue "@var{text}"
dialog "@var{text}"
@end example

Displays a dialogue message or message box.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
dialogue("Welcome to the game!")
dialog("Press any key to continue")
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Print expressions: any value (converted to string)
@item Input variables: must be valid identifiers
@item String concatenation: use @code{..} operator
@end itemize

@node Assignment statements
@section Assignment Statements

@findex local
@findex =
@findex move
@findex to

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Assignment statements store values into variables, table fields,
or other storage locations.

@subsubheading Simple Assignment

@example
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
local @var{variable} = @var{expression}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
score = 0
lives = 3
player_name = "Hero"
counter = counter + 1
local result = calculate(x, y)
@end example

@subsubheading Move Statement

@example
move @var{expression} to @var{variable}
@end example

Copies the value of expression to variable.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
move 100 to score
move temp to final
move x * 2 to result
@end example

@subsubheading Table Assignment

@example
@var{table}[@var{key}] = @var{value}
@var{table}.@var{key} = @var{value}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
items[1] = 100
items["name"] = "Item"
player.health = 100
config["difficulty"] = "hard"
@end example

@subsubheading Multiple Assignment

@example
@var{a}, @var{b}, @var{c} = @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{expr3}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
x, y = y, x                    -- swap
min, max = math.min(a,b), math.max(a,b)
a, b, c = 1, 2                  -- c is nil
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Variable names: valid identifiers
@item Table keys: any non-nil value
@item Values: any Lua value
@end itemize

@node Routine calls
@section Routine Calls

@findex function
@findex return
@findex :

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Routine calls invoke functions, optionally passing arguments and receiving
return values. Lua supports methods via the colon syntax.

@subsubheading Nullary Call

@example
@var{function}()
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
initializeGame()
clearScreen()
playStartupMusic()
@end example

@subsubheading Call with Arguments

@example
@var{function}(@var{arg1}, @var{arg2}, @var{arg3})
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
drawSprite(sprite_id, x, y)
loadLevel(level_number)
calculate_score(player_id, time_bonus)
@end example

@subsubheading Method Invocation

@example
@var{object}:@var{method}(@var{args})
@end example

The colon automatically passes @code{self} as the first argument.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
player:move(5, 10)
enemy:takeDamage(50)
ui:updateDisplay()
@end example

@subsubheading Table Functions

Functions stored in tables can be called like methods:

@example
table.insert(items, value)
table.remove(items, index)
string.format("%d + %d = %d", a, b, c)
@end example

@subsubheading Return Values

@example
return
return @var{expression}
return @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{...}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
return
return score
return level * 100, bonus
return true, "success"
@end example

@node Control flow statements
@section Control Flow Statements

@findex return
@findex break
@findex continue

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Control flow statements transfer execution or exit from blocks.

@subsubheading Return Statement

@example
return
return @var{expression}
return @var{expr1}, @var{expr2}, @var{...}
@end example

Exits the current function, optionally returning values.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
function findItem(list, target)
    for i, v in ipairs(list) do
        if v == target then
            return i  -- return index
        end
    end
    return nil  -- not found
end
@end example

@subsubheading Break Statement

@example
break
@end example

Exits the innermost enclosing loop.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
while true do
    event = getNextEvent()
    if event == nil then
        break
    end
    process(event)
end
@end example

@subsubheading Continue Statement

@example
continue
@end example

Skips to the next iteration of the enclosing loop.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
for i = 1, 100 do
    if i % 7 == 0 then
        continue  -- skip multiples of 7
    end
    process(i)
end
@end example

@node Arithmetic operations
@section Arithmetic Operations

@findex +
@findex -
@findex *
@findex /
@findex %
@findex ^

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Arithmetic operations perform numeric calculations on operands.

@subsubheading Addition

@example
result = @var{a} + @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
total = price + tax
score = score + 10
x = left_margin + offset
@end example

@subsubheading Subtraction

@example
result = @var{a} - @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
balance = total - deductions
health = health - damage
x = x - speed
@end example

@subsubheading Multiplication

@example
result = @var{a} * @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
area = width * height
total = price * quantity
interest = principal * rate * years
@end example

@subsubheading Division

@example
result = @var{a} / @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
average = sum / count
speed = distance / time
ratio = width / height
@end example

@subsubheading Modulo

@example
result = @var{a} % @var{b}
@end example

Returns the remainder of integer division.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
remainder = 17 % 5     -- 2
index = (i - 1) % 10 + 1
@end example

@subsubheading Exponentiation

@example
result = @var{a} ^ @var{b}
@end example

Raises @var{a} to the power of @var{b}.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
squared = x ^ 2
volume = length ^ 3
distance = ((x2 - x1) ^ 2 + (y2 - y1) ^ 2) ^ 0.5
@end example

@subsubheading Unary Minus

@example
result = -@var{a}
@end example

@subsubheading Examples

@example
negated = -value
distance = -offset
@end example

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item All operands: numeric values
@item Division by zero: results in error or infinity
@item Modulo: second operand must not be zero
@end itemize

@subsubheading Bitwise Operations

Lua 5.2+ includes the bit32 library:

@example
local bit32 = require("bit32")
result = bit32.lshift(value, 2)   -- left shift
result = bit32.rshift(value, 1)   -- right shift
result = bit32.band(x, y)         -- bitwise AND
result = bit32.bor(x, y)          -- bitwise OR
result = bit32.bxor(x, y)         -- bitwise XOR
@end example

@node Boolean expressions
@section Boolean Expressions

@findex ==
@findex ~=
@findex <
@findex >
@findex <=
@findex >=
@findex and
@findex or
@findex not
@findex true
@findex false
@findex nil

@subsubheading Purpose and Function

Boolean expressions evaluate conditions and combine multiple tests.

@subsubheading Equality

@example
@var{a} == @var{b}
@end example

True if @var{a} equals @var{b}.

@subsubheading Inequality

@example
@var{a} ~= @var{b}
@end example

True if @var{a} does not equal @var{b}.

@subsubheading Less Than

@example
@var{a} < @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Greater Than

@example
@var{a} > @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Less Than or Equal

@example
@var{a} <= @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Greater Than or Equal

@example
@var{a} >= @var{b}
@end example

@subsubheading Logical AND

@example
@var{a} and @var{b}
@end example

Returns first operand if falsy, otherwise returns second operand.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if score > 100 and lives > 0 then
    continueGame()
end

-- Short-circuit
if func1() and func2() then
    print("Both succeeded")
end
@end example

@subsubheading Logical OR

@example
@var{a} or @var{b}
@end example

Returns first operand if truthy, otherwise returns second operand.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
-- Default value
speed = speed or 100

-- Conditional assignment
if choice == "yes" or choice == "y" then
    proceed()
end
@end example

@subsubheading Logical NOT

@example
not @var{expression}
@end example

Returns true if expression is falsy, false if truthy.

@subsubheading Examples

@example
if not gameOver then
    updateGame()
end

if not (x < min or x > max) then
    inRange = true
end
@end example

@subsubheading Truth Values

@itemize @bullet
@item Falsy: @code{nil}, @code{false}
@item Truthy: everything else (0, "", tables, functions, etc.)
@end itemize

@subsubheading Valid Ranges

@itemize @bullet
@item Comparison operands: compatible types
@item Mixing types in comparison: may produce unexpected results
@end itemize

@subsubheading Operator Precedence

Highest to lowest:
@code{^}, @code{not}, @code{*}, @code{/}, @code{%},
@code{+}, @code{-}, @code{..}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=},
@code{==}, @code{~=}, @code{and}, @code{or}

@node Unsupported features
@section Unsupported Features

The following Lua features are not supported in this EIGHTBOL release:

@table @asis
@item Standard libraries
File I/O (io.*), OS functions (os.*), debug library, math library
(beyond basic operations)

@item Coroutines
@code{coroutine.*}, @code{yield}, @code{resume}

@item Modules and require
@code{require}, @code{module}, @code{package.*}

@item Metatables and metamethods
Advanced OOP patterns using metatables

@item Iterators and generic for
Custom iterators using @code{__pairs}, @code{__ipairs}

@item Vararg expressions
@code{...}, @code{select()}, @code{arg} table

@item Garbage collection control
@code{collectgarbage()}

@item Environments
@code{_G}, @code{setfenv()}, @code{getfenv()}

@item Weak tables
@code{weak} mode in metatables

@item Variadic functions
Functions with variable arguments (though limited support via
fixed parameters)
@end table

@node Lua
